cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use agent-runtime-integrity-bench in research or software, please cite it as below."
title: "agent-runtime-integrity-bench: integrity scenarios for agent runtimes"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Dziatkovskii
    given-names: Anton
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7408-3054"
    affiliation: "Palo Alto AI Research Lab"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Palo-Alto-AI-Research-Lab/agent-runtime-integrity-bench"
url: "https://github.com/Palo-Alto-AI-Research-Lab/agent-runtime-integrity-bench"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - benchmark
  - concurrency
  - idempotency
  - silent failure
  - agent memory
abstract: >-
  A benchmark that injects faults and checks runtime invariants directly, rather
  than measuring task success — because the agent-runtime failures that matter are
  silent, with every component reporting success while an invariant is dead.
  Scenarios are modelled on dated incidents from a production multi-machine agent
  fleet: concurrent memory writes (lost updates, close-races, silent state
  resurrection after close) and replay/idempotency (a redelivered batch applied
  twice). Verdicts are held / violated / error / not_applicable, with abstention a
  first-class outcome, and the harness self-tests against seven mutants to prove it
  can tell good from bad. Published results cover four openai-agents session
  backends, reproduced identically on macOS and Linux.
